      "text": "   - Note that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always $180^\\circ$.\n   - From a given diagram, we find that the measure of $\\angle ABC$ is $93^\\circ$.\n   - Contrasting with the given triangle $ABC$, there is an additional angle at point $D$, denoted as $130^\\circ$, which is an external angle for triangle $ABC$.\n   - Consequently, we calculate $m\\angle ACB$, using the fact that an exterior angle in a triangle is equal to the sum of the two interior angles not adjacent to it.\n   - Given that the measure of $\\angle ABC$ and $\\angle ACB$ are $93^\\circ$ and $130^\\circ$ respectively, we can write the following equation: $$93^\\circ + m\\angle ACB = 180^\\circ$$\n   - Solving for $m\\angle ACB$, we get $$m\\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 93^\\circ = 137^\\circ$$",
